  scenarioId: scenario-05
  codeText: "#include \"stdio.h\"\n\nint add(int a, int b)\n{\n    return (a + b);\n};\n\nint main()\n{\n    int y = add(5,\"six\");\n\n    printf(\"%d\", y);\n}"
  codeImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 93637bd6a3d63b8d886c8313e30011be, type: 3}
  rawFeedbackImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 08b10520a0074da7f8c1746a8874872e, type: 3}
  neutralFeedbackImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 3b4e77d152aa658399ea4b0415fede66, type: 3}
  meowraFeedbackImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 14a4256a22a1600c09da730ca11970e9, type: 3}
  question: "Why does the second argument in add(5, \"six\") cause a type mismatch?"
  answerA: "The second parameter expects an int, but \"six\" is a string literal."
  answerB: "The function accepts only one argument, but the call supplies two."
  answerC: "The second parameter expects a string, but \"six\" is an int."
  answerD: "The function returns an int, so it cannot accept any arguments."
  correctAnswer: 0
  rawFeedback: "function_call.c: In function ‘main’:\nfunction_call.c:10:19: error: passing argument 2 of ‘add’ makes integer from pointer without a cast [-Wint-conversion]\n   10 |     int y = add(5,\"six\");\n      |                   ^~~~~\n      |                   |\n      |                   char *\nfunction_call.c:3:20: note: expected ‘int’ but argument is of type ‘char *’\n    3 | int add(int a, int b)\n      |                ~~~~^"
  neutralFeedback: "function_call.c:10:19: error: The second argument passed to 'add' does not match the type the function expects. Check the function parameters and make sure each argument uses the expected type.\n   10 |     int y = add(5,\"six\");\n      |                   ^~~~~"
  meowraFeedback: "function_call.c:10:19: error: The second argument passed to 'add' does not match the type the function expects, nya. Check the function parameters and make sure each argument uses the expected type.\n   10 |     int y = add(5,\"six\");\n      |                   ^~~~~"
